## 4. We ran four Claude configurations

The current critical-screen comparison uses Replit-managed Anthropic access, without a personal-key fallback.

| Configuration | Reasoning setting | Output token cap |
|---|---|---:|
| Sonnet 5.5 baseline | Provider default | 8,000 |
| Sonnet 5.5 maximum | Adaptive, maximum effort | 16,000 |
| Opus 5.5 medium | Adaptive, medium effort | 16,000 |
| Opus 5.5 maximum | Adaptive, maximum effort | 16,000 |

**Rationale:**
- **Sonnet baseline** provides a faster reference point.
- **Sonnet maximum** tests whether more reasoning improves the same model’s screening.
- **Opus medium** tests a larger model without the maximum reasoning cost.
- **Opus maximum** tests whether the most intensive configuration improves coverage enough to justify its additional time and cost.

We kept the screening prompts and data fixed and recorded configuration fingerprints to make changes detectable.

**Important qualification:** this is a comparison of complete configurations, not a perfectly isolated experiment on reasoning effort. The baseline also has a different output cap. A token cap is a maximum allowance—not the number of tokens necessarily used.

The current critical-screen comparison does **not** include a new OpenAI run; the earlier OpenAI work belongs to the broader human-agreement study.

