## 8. What the completed results show

### At the original fixed threshold of 12

| Model | Critical episodes caught | Noncritical episodes incorrectly flagged |
|---|---:|---:|
| Sonnet baseline | **17 / 26 — 65.4%** | **56 / 274 — 20.4%** |
| Opus medium | **21 / 26 — 80.8%** | **112 / 274 — 40.9%** |

**Interpretation:** Opus medium catches more critical cases at this threshold, but produces far too many false flags for the chosen constraint. Sonnet baseline also narrowly exceeds 20%.

This demonstrates why comparing models at the same numeric threshold is not enough: their score distributions differ.

### After held-out threshold calibration targeting a 20% false-flag cap

| Model | Critical episodes caught | Critical episodes missed | Noncritical episodes incorrectly flagged |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Sonnet baseline | **15 / 26 — 57.7%** | **11 / 26 — 42.3%** | **51 / 274 — 18.6%** |
| Opus medium | **16 / 26 — 61.5%** | **10 / 26 — 38.5%** | **49 / 274 — 17.9%** |

**Interpretation:** Opus medium currently catches **one more critical episode** and generates **two fewer false flags**.

That is a modest observed advantage, not proof of a substantial or statistically reliable improvement. There are only 26 critical episodes, so one episode changes detection by approximately **3.8 percentage points**.

Neither configuration is yet catching all, or nearly all, critical cases under the selected constraint.

### Maximum-thinking models
- Sonnet maximum: **284 valid, 16 invalid**
- Opus maximum: **286 valid, 14 invalid**

We have withheld a final calibrated ranking for them.

**Rationale:** evaluating only the valid outputs could bias the comparison, especially if difficult or critical episodes are more likely to produce invalid responses.

